Exercise 2.4:

A simple use of @volatile is a shared pause flag controlling 
whether instruction execution is temporarily suspended. 
Multiple worker threads repeatedly check this variable before continuing execution. 
Without @volatile, threads may cache the variable locally and fail to observe updates performed 
by another thread, causing some instructions to remain blocked indefinitely.

By declaring the variable as @volatile, all writes become immediately visible to other threads, 
guaranteeing that paused worker threads correctly resume execution after the flag changes.

in VolatileTest.scala we:

Call /pause
Submit /run-simulation with the dependency-rich input program
Wait 1.5s so workers enter the paused loop
Call /resume
Fetch /status and print it


This scenario is a pure visibility problem: one thread writes paused=false, other threads are busy-reading paused
@volatile gives the happens-before visibility guarantee needed for this pattern